#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# O

#% text
# `O(x^n)` stands for the order term of a printed power series: terms of order `x^n` and higher.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `o(x)` is the order term `O(x)` as a value: a series in x with no terms; `o(x)^k` is `O(x^k)` and `f + o(x)^k` is the series of f to that order. `o(x, x0)` is `O(x - x0)`.

#% item
# A series prints with its order term; `series(... + O(x^n), x, 0, k)` reads such an expression back as a series.

#% item
# `normal` drops the order term.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
series(exp(x), x, 0, 3)
#% output
#= 1 + x + x^2/2 + x^3/6 + O(x^4)

#% cell
series(1 + x + x^2 + O(x^3), x, 0, 2)
#% output
#= 1 + x + x^2 + O(x^3)

#% cell
normal(series(1/(1 - x), x, 0, 4))
#% output
#= 1 + x + x^2 + x^3 + x^4

#% subsection
# Scope

#% cell
x + x^2/2 + o(x)^10
#% output
#= x + x^2/2 + O(x^10)

#% cell
1/(1 + exp(x + o(x)^5))
#% output
#= 1/2 - x/4 + x^3/48 + O(x^5)

#% cell
exp(x) + o(x, 1)^3
#% output
#= e + e*(x - 1) + (1/2)*e*(x - 1)^2 + O((x - 1)^3)

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Arithmetic of series keeps the smaller order:

#% cell
series(sin(x), x, 0, 5)*series(cos(x), x, 0, 3)
#% output
#= x - 2*x^3/3 + O(x^5)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `series`, `normal`, `o`
